Set $\kappa=1/10000$. For every fixed $\beta>1$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P_{J,\,x_0\sim\pi^J}
  \left\{d^J\bigl(x_0,\exp(n^\kappa)\bigr)>\frac14\right\}
  &\longrightarrow1,\\
 \mathbb P_{J,\,x_0\sim\pi^J}
  \left\{d_{\mathrm{disc}}^J
       \bigl(x_0,\lfloor\exp(n^\kappa)\rfloor\bigr)>\frac14\right\}
  &\longrightarrow1.
\end{aligned}$$

Let $G$ be a standard Gaussian vector in a finite-dimensional Euclidean space. If $f$ is $L$-Lipschitz, then for $a>0$ and $\theta\in\mathbb R$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P\bigl[|f(G)-\mathbb Ef(G)|\ge a\bigr]
 &\le 2\exp\left(-\frac{a^2}{2L^2}\right),
 \label{static-eq:gaussian-concentration}\\
 \log\mathbb E\exp\{\theta(f(G)-\mathbb Ef(G))\}
 &\le\frac{\theta^2L^2}{2}.
 \label{static-eq:gaussian-mgf}
\end{aligned}$$ If a probability density on $\mathbb R^m$ is proportional to $e^{-V}$, where $V$ is twice differentiable and $\nabla^2V\succeq I$, then $$\label{static-eq:brascamp-lieb}
 \mathop{\mathrm{Var}}(f)\le\mathbb E\|\nabla f\|^2.$$ In particular, the variance of a linear functional $a\cdot G$ under this density is at most $\|a\|^2$.

For every feasible $D$ and every path and multiplier as above, $$\label{static-eq:matrix-bound}
 F_{d,n}(D)\le
 \Psi(Q,x,\lambda)-\lambda:D
 +\frac{\beta^2}{4}
       \left(|D-Q_{\rm end}|^2-\int x\,\,\mathrm d|Q|^2\right).$$ For fixed $D$ and $\lambda$, the bound passes to any sequence of finite paths for which $Q_{\rm end}$, $\Psi(Q,x,\lambda)$, and the displayed path integral converge.

Let $\Delta_{\min}=\min_j\Delta_j$. If $k\ge1$ and $k/\sqrt{n\Delta_{\min}}\le1$, then $$\label{static-eq:average-overlap-concentration}
 \sum_{j=0}^kw_j\mathbb E_j^{(2)}(R-r_j)^2
 \le C\left(\frac{k}{\sqrt{n\Delta_{\min}}}+k^{-1/2}\right),$$ where $C$ is an absolute constant.

The step approximation defining $\Phi_\alpha$ is independent of the approximating sequence. The functions $\Phi_\alpha,u,\chi$ are continuous on $[0,1]\times\mathbb R$, with $$\label{static-eq:scalar-derivative-bounds}
 |u|\le1,\qquad 0<\chi\le1,\qquad
 |\chi(s,z)-\chi(s,z')|\le C_\beta|z-z'|.$$ The function $\Phi_\alpha(s,\cdot)$ is even, so $u(s,0)=0$. For two distribution functions $\alpha,\widetilde\alpha$, $$\label{static-eq:scalar-stability}
 \sup_{s,z}\bigl(
 |\Phi_\alpha-\Phi_{\widetilde\alpha}|
 +|u_\alpha-u_{\widetilde\alpha}|
 +|\chi_\alpha-\chi_{\widetilde\alpha}|
 \bigr)
 \le C_\beta\int_0^1|\alpha(s)-\widetilde\alpha(s)|\,\mathrm ds.$$ These functions are also continuous in $\beta>0$, uniformly in $(s,z)$ for their differences, locally in $\beta$.*






*The stochastic differential equation $$\label{static-eq:scalar-diffusion}
 \,\mathrm dX_s=\beta\,\mathrm dB_s+\beta^2\alpha(s)u(s,X_s)\,\mathrm ds,
 \qquad X_0=0,$$ has a unique strong solution. For a step distribution function, its transitions across the constant-mass intervals are the normalized tilted Gaussian kernels of the scalar recursion. Its bounded martingale $U_s=u(s,X_s)$ satisfies $$\label{static-eq:scalar-martingale}
 U_t-U_s=\beta\int_s^t\chi(v,X_v)\,\mathrm dB_v.$$ Moreover, $$\label{static-eq:scalar-chi-expectation}
 \mathbb E\chi(t,X_t)-\mathbb E\chi(s,X_s)
 =-\beta^2\int_s^t\alpha(v)\mathbb E\chi(v,X_v)^2\,\mathrm dv.$$ For $0\le s<t\le1$, the conditional form is $$\label{eq:scalar-evolution}
 \mathbb E[\chi(t,X_t)\mid X_s]
 =\chi(s,X_s)-\beta^2\int_s^t\alpha(v)
                   \mathbb E[\chi(v,X_v)^2\mid X_s]\,\mathrm dv.$$ Consequently $\Gamma(s):=\mathbb Eu(s,X_s)^2$ is continuously differentiable, with one-sided derivatives at the endpoints, and $$\label{static-eq:gamma-derivative}
 \Gamma(0)=0,\qquad
 \Gamma'(s)=\beta^2\mathbb E\chi(s,X_s)^2.$$ The well-posedness statement and the evolution identities <a href="#static-eq:scalar-martingale" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="static-eq:scalar-martingale">[static-eq:scalar-martingale]</a>–<a href="#static-eq:scalar-chi-expectation" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="static-eq:scalar-chi-expectation">[static-eq:scalar-chi-expectation]</a> also hold for a diffusion started at any deterministic time and spatial value.

The scalar functional has a minimizer $\mu$. For every such minimizer, with its associated scalar process, the following assertions hold:*

1.  *$0\in\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu$, and $\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu\subset[0,1-\varepsilon_0]$ for some $\varepsilon_0>0$.*

2.  *At every $q\in\mathop{\mathrm{supp}}\mu\cap(0,1)$, $\Gamma(q)=q$ and $\Gamma'(q)\le1$. At $q=0$, $\Gamma(0)=0$ and $\Gamma'(0)\le1$.*





3.  *Define $$\label{static-eq:scalar-A-d-definitions}
       d_*(s)=1-\int_{(s,1]}t\,\mu(\,\mathrm dt),\qquad
       A(s)=\int_s^1\alpha(t)\,\mathrm dt=d_*(s)-s\alpha(s).$$ Then, at every $s\in[0,1]$, $$\label{static-eq:scalar-stationarity-identity}
       \mathbb E\bigl[\chi(s,X_s)+\alpha(s)u(s,X_s)^2\bigr]=d_*(s).$$ Equivalently, since $d_*(s)=A(s)+s\alpha(s)$, $$\label{eq:scalar-stationarity}
       \mathbb E\chi(s,X_s)=A(s)+\alpha(s)(s-\Gamma(s))
           \qquad(0\le s\le1).$$ In particular $$\label{static-eq:scalar-susceptibility}
       \chi(0,0)=A(0),\qquad \beta A(0)\le1.$$ Furthermore $\alpha(s)>0$ for every $s\in(0,1]$, $A(h)<A(0)$ for every $h\in(0,1]$, and $d_*(0+)=A(0)$.

For each fixed $\beta>1$, there are $e_0=e_0(\beta)<\beta/2$ and $N=N(\beta)$ such that $$\label{static-eq:energy-gap}
 p_n'(\beta)=\frac1n\mathbb E\pi_{n,\beta}^J(H_n^J)\le e_0
 \qquad(n\ge N).$$

Let $e_*=1/100$. For every fixed $h\in(0,1)$, there is $b>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{eq:fixed-locking}
 \mathbb E\pi^{\otimes3}\!\left\{
 \begin{gathered}
 R(x,y)\ge h,\qquad |R(v,x)|,|R(v,y)|\le b,\\
 |R(v,x)-R(v,y)|>2n^{-e_*}
 \end{gathered}\right\}\le e^{-n^{9/10}}.$$ The same conclusion holds after decreasing $b$.

Fix a replica number $d$. If a feasible overlap matrix $D$ satisfies $$F_{d,n}(D)\le dP_\beta-\eta,
 \qquad \eta\ge2dC_\beta n^{-1/24},$$ where $C_\beta$ is the constant in Theorem <a href="#static-thm:finite-size" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="static-thm:finite-size">6</a>, then, for all sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{static-eq:pressure-gap-probability}
 \mathbb E\frac{Z_{d,n}(D)}{Z_{n,\beta}^d}
 \le \exp(-n\eta/4)+\exp(-c_{\beta,d}n\eta^2).$$ The constants are uniform over $D$ and over $n$-dependent gaps satisfying the displayed lower bound.

For every nonempty pair constraint with overlap $q\in[0,1]$, $$\label{static-eq:pair-pressure}
 F_{2,n}\!\left(\begin{smallmatrix}1&q\\q&1\end{smallmatrix}\right)
 \leq 2P_\beta-\frac12\bigl(\Gamma(q)-q\bigr)^2.$$ Consequently, for every fixed $\tau>0$, the averaged Gibbs probability of pairs satisfying $\bigl|\Gamma(|R(x,y)|)-|R(x,y)|\bigr|>\tau$ is at most $C\exp(-cn)$ for all large $n$.

For every fixed $h\in(0,1)$ there is $b>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{static-eq:locking-probability}
 \mathbb E\pi^{\otimes3}\!\left\{
 \begin{gathered}
 |R(x,y)|\ge h,\qquad |R(v,x)|,|R(v,y)|\le b,\\
 \bigl||R(v,x)|-|R(v,y)|\bigr|>2n^{-1/100}
 \end{gathered}\right\}\le \exp(-n^{9/10}).$$ The same conclusion holds after decreasing $b$.

There are constants $e_1\in(0,\beta/2)$ and $c_E>0$ such that, for all sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{static-eq:coverage-energy-good}
 \mathbb P_J\!\left(\pi^J\{H^J>ne_1\}>e^{-c_E n}\right)
 \le e^{-c_E n}.$$

Let $J,G$ be independent standard Gaussian vectors and, for $\theta\in(0,\pi/2)$, set $J_\theta=\cos\theta J+\sin\theta G$. Then for every $\lambda\in\mathbb R$, $$\label{static-eq:coverage-rotation-mgf}
 \mathbb E\exp\!\left\{\lambda(\log Z^{J_\theta}-\log Z^J)\right\}
 \le\exp\!\left\{\frac{\lambda^2\theta^2\beta^2(n-1)}4\right\}.$$ In particular, for every $u>0$, $$\label{static-eq:coverage-rotation-tail}
 \mathbb P\!\left(\log Z^{J_\theta}-\log Z^J\ge u\right)
 \le\exp\!\left\{-\frac{u^2}{\theta^2\beta^2(n-1)}\right\}.$$

Suppose that $$\label{eq:narrow-identity}
 \Gamma(s)=s\qquad\text{for }t_0/2<s<3t_0.$$ For three independent Gibbs configurations $v,x,y$, conditionally on the disorder, define $$q=R(x,y),\qquad
 r=\frac{R(v,x)+R(v,y)}2,\qquad
 \delta=\frac{R(v,x)-R(v,y)}2.$$ Then, for all sufficiently large $n$, $$\label{eq:narrow-locking-probability}
 \mathbb E\pi^{\otimes3}\left\{
 \begin{array}{c}
 t_0/2<r<3t_0,\quad \mu([r-h,r+h])\le\rho^4,\\
 q\ge r+\rho,\quad \varepsilon\le|\delta|\le2\varepsilon
 \end{array}\right\}
 \le \exp(-n^{4/5}).$$

There exists $q_*=q_*(\beta)\in(0,1)$ such that, for every fixed $D_1>0$, there exists a fixed $D_2>0$ for which $$\label{poly-eq:coverage-uniform}
 \mathbb P_J\!\left(
  \text{for every }S\subset\{-1,1\}^n\text{ with }\pi^J(S)\ge n^{-D_1},\quad
  (\pi_S^J)^{\otimes2}\{|R|\ge q_*\}\ge n^{-D_2}
 \right)\longrightarrow1.$$ The threshold $q_*$ does not depend on $D_1$.

Let $q_*$ be as in Proposition <a href="#poly-prop:coverage" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="poly-prop:coverage">20</a>. For every fixed $D_1>0$, there exists a fixed $D_3>0$ such that, with probability tending to one over $J$, every $S\subset\{-1,1\}^n$ satisfies $$\label{poly-eq:coverage-cells}
 \pi^J\!\left(
  \left\{x\in S:
   \pi^J\{b\in S:|R(x,b)|\ge q_*\}<n^{-D_3}
  \right\}\right)<n^{-D_1}.$$

For every fixed $\beta>1$ and every fixed $a>0$, $$\begin{aligned}
 \mathbb P_{J,\,x_0\sim\pi^J}
 \left\{d^J(x_0,n^a)>\frac14\right\}&\longrightarrow1,
 \label{fixed-eq:continuous-conclusion}\\
 \mathbb P_{J,\,x_0\sim\pi^J}
 \left\{d_{\mathrm{disc}}^J(x_0,\lfloor n^a\rfloor)
                                      >\frac14\right\}
 &\longrightarrow1.
 \label{fixed-eq:discrete-conclusion}
\end{aligned}$$ Consequently both $\mathbb P\{T^J(x_0)>n^a\}$ and $\mathbb P\{T_{\mathrm{disc}}^J(x_0)>n^a\}$ tend to one under the same joint law. Each assertion is equivalently a statement that the Gibbs mass of the indicated initial states tends to one in probability over $J$.
